## 4. <a href="https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm032025-supply-chain/" target="_blank">LLM03 – Supply Chain Vulnerabilities</a>

### 4.1 Context in the Lyra System

In a full ecosystem, the following elements are updated over time:

- core LLM models and embeddings
- Python libraries and SDKs
- Docker images and base OS images
- STT/TTS engines and models
- face recognition and CV libraries (e.g., OpenCV, ONNX, Mediapipe)
- integration modules for maps, flight APIs, calendars, etc.

Each update is a potential **supply chain entry point**.

### 4.2 Risk Characteristics

**Impact (I)** if a supply chain component is compromised:

- code execution on Lyra or Vega
- malicious instructions sent to IoT or automation systems
- tampered models or embeddings affecting decisions
- compromised API clients returning manipulated data
- RAG ingestion of malicious or manipulated content

This may require a full rebuild and trust reset of Lyra and dependent components.

**Probability (P)**  
Annual probability that:

- a model, library, or image pulled from upstream is malicious or compromised  
- and that it is deployed into the running system before detection

Each new integration adds an additional tool edge; even with pinned versions, some risk remains.

### 4.3 Risk Object Definition

```text
ID: LLM03
Title: Malicious Model/Library/Tool Introduced via Update
Description: A compromised model, dependency, or container introduces malicious behavior or remote code execution into the Lyra ecosystem.
Impact Definition: Rebuild and verification cost across Lyra, Deb, and other affected systems, including potential data loss remediation.
Probability Definition: Annual chance of deploying a compromised dependency in normal maintenance or feature work.
Inputs Needed:
    i_min:
    i_mode:
    i_max:
    p_alpha:
    p_beta:
Mitigations:
  - Version pinning and reproducible builds
  - Internal mirrors or caches for trusted artifacts
  - Network isolation for runtime containers
  - Periodic security reviews of third-party code
Residual Notes:
  - Low-frequency, high-impact; one of the largest catastrophic risk categories.
```

