## 5. <a href="https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm042025-data-and-model-poisoning/" target="_blank">LLM04 – Model / Data Poisoning</a>

### 5.1 Context in the Lyra System

As Lyra’s RAG and memory expand:

- corpus grows large over time
- multiple sources feed the vector database
- ingestion pipelines may be partly or fully automated
- household notes and documentation are stored in RAG
- camera perception may generate embeddings or tags
- sensors provide streaming data that is summarized
- external references and URLs may be imported

“Poisoning” includes both malicious and accidental contamination, as well as stale data being treated as current truth.

### 5.2 Risk Characteristics

**Impact (I)** may include:

- incorrect system or network configurations
- degraded assistant quality and reliability
- persistent misinformation anchored in stored memory
- bad reasoning loops anchored in corrupted context
- long-term contamination of memory that requires audit and cleanup
- reindexing or partial rebuild of the vector store

**Probability (P)**  
Annual probability that:

- incorrect or misleading data enters RAG or long-term memory and materially influences decisions before being detected

The risk increases significantly as ingestion becomes more automated and the corpus grows.

### 5.3 Risk Object Definition

```text
ID: LLM04
Title: RAG Memory Poisoning
Description: Lyra ingests incorrect, stale, or manipulated documents into the vector store or memory, causing persistent distortion of reasoning and guidance.
Impact Definition: Cost of incorrect decisions based on contaminated memory plus cost to diagnose, purge, and re-index the affected data.
Probability Definition: Annual chance of harmful ingestion of misleading or stale content that influences behavior.
Inputs Needed:
    i_min:
    i_mode:
    i_max:
    p_alpha:
    p_beta:
Mitigations:
  - Manual curation and approval of ingress sources
  - Separation of “draft” vs “trusted” document pools
  - Regular audits of high-impact documents
Residual Notes:
  - Likely to be a significant recurring contributor to ALE.
```

