## 6. <a href="https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm052025-improper-output-handling/" target="_blank">LLM05 – Improper Output Handling</a>

### 6.1 Context in the Lyra System

As Lyra gains more tools, she may output:

- HTML or Markdown
- JSON or structured data
- shell snippets or configuration fragments
- commands for local tools
- UI components or layout instructions

If front-end systems (MagicMirror, dashboards, admin panels) render this output without proper escaping or validation, risks include:

- cross-site scripting (XSS)
- HTML/UI injection
- log poisoning
- configuration injection, if text is written into config files directly
- unintended file or command execution in automation pipelines

### 6.2 Risk Characteristics

**Impact (I)**  
Potentially up to:

- local browser or UI compromise
- configuration corruption
- local admin sessions affected
- manual cleanup and configuration repair

Higher if Lyra output is ever fed into automation paths without human review.

**Probability (P)**  
Annual probability that:

- a malformed or adversarial output string  
- is rendered or applied in a context that executes or interprets it unsafely

Rises if the UI supports rich rendering or if Lyra output is used as-is in downstream processes.

### 6.3 Risk Object Definition

```text
ID: LLM05
Title: Unsafe Rendering of Lyra Output in UI
Description: Output containing markup or scripts is rendered unsafely in a UI or used directly in configs, leading to local injection or corruption.
Impact Definition: Cost to correct UI or configuration corruption and handle any local compromise.
Probability Definition: Annual chance that unsafe output is consumed by a rendering or automation component without sanitization.
Inputs Needed:
    i_min:
    i_mode:
    i_max:
    p_alpha:
    p_beta:
Mitigations:
  - Strict plain-text rendering for general responses
  - Explicit escaping for structured outputs
  - Separate “preview and approve” step before applying configs
Residual Notes:
  - Risk grows with richer UI behavior and automation pathways.
```

