## 8. <a href="https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm072025-system-prompt-leakage/" target="_blank">LLM07 – System Prompt Leakage</a>

### 8.1 Context the Lyra System

The system prompt and configuration may include:

- memory keys and schema descriptions
- safety rules and constraint schemas
- persona ethics and behavioral norms
- tool routing patterns and allowed actions
- instructions for handling policy or safety questions
- references to `house_profile` and other structured sources
- mappings to governance frameworks

Although not housing passwords or secrets, leakage of these internals provides an attacker or adversarial user with **blueprints** for jailbreak strategies and targeted manipulations.

### 8.2 Risk Characteristics

**Impact (I)** can include:

- increased success rate of prompt injection attempts
- destabilization of persona and safety behavior
- need to rotate, redesign, and revalidate prompts and governance schemas
- time spent on remedial design and testing

**Probability (P)**  
Annual probability that:

- internal instructions or routing hints are exposed via conversations, logs, or debug tools

Grows with the number of users, complexity of interactions, and debugging/introspection features.

### 8.3 Risk Object Definition

```text
ID: LLM07
Title: Exposure of System Prompt & Governance Rules
Description: System prompts, routing logic, and governance instructions are exposed, enabling targeted manipulation or jailbreak tuning.
Impact Definition: Cost of revising prompts, updating governance schemas, and revalidating behavior, plus any knock-on increase in other risks.
Probability Definition: Annual chance of leakage through logs, UI, or interactions.
Inputs Needed:
    i_min:
    i_mode:
    i_max:
    p_alpha:
    p_beta:
Mitigations:
  - Avoid exposing internal instructions verbatim
  - Sanitize logs and debug output
  - Limit access to introspection tools
Residual Notes:
  - Acts as a multiplier for other risks rather than a standalone catastrophic event.
```

