## <a href="https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm102025-unbounded-consumption/" target="_blank">11. LLM10 – Unbounded Consumption (Resource Attacks)</a>

### 11.1 Context in the Lyra System

A future Lyra may face:

- loops in STT → LLM → TTS cycles
- vision loops or constant camera streaming
- multiple Pi nodes and sensors emitting frequent events
- high token-output tasks (summaries, large docs)
- RAG-heavy workflows
- long-running chains of external tool calls
- runaway logs or unbounded telemetry

This can lead to resource exhaustion:

- GPU VRAM spikes
- CPU saturation
- memory leaks in gateway or RAG processes
- infinite or deeply nested tool recursion
- MQTT or event-bus flooding

### 11.2 Risk Characteristics

**Impact (I)** includes:

- temporary service degradation or unavailability
- container restarts and log cleanup
- diagnostic time to locate and fix loops
- potential impact on other household services using the same hardware

**Probability (P)**  
Annual probability that:

- a runaway loop or high-load pattern is triggered by user requests, misconfiguration, or bugs in orchestration

This grows with feature complexity and automation density.

### 11.3 Risk Object Definition

```text
ID: LLM10
Title: Runaway Resource Consumption
Description: Recursive workloads, unbounded responses, or sensor overload saturate CPU/GPU/memory, causing downtime or degraded service.
Impact Definition: Downtime and recovery cost, including restarts, cleanup, and diagnosis.
Probability Definition: Annual chance of uncontrolled resource use due to loops, misconfiguration, or bugs.
Inputs Needed:
    i_min:
    i_mode:
    i_max:
    p_alpha:
    p_beta:
Mitigations:
  - Token and context-length limits
  - Tool call depth limits
  - Rate limiting for external and internal calls
  - Timeouts and circuit-breakers for tools
Residual Notes:
  - Typically nuisance-level per incident, but can be frequent.
```

